---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Greeting from '../components/Greeting';
const skills = [
'JS/TS',
'React',
'HTML Canvas',
'HTML/CSS',
'Docker',
'Rest API/OpenAPI',
'Java',
'Python/Django',
'Learning new things really fast',
'Vim/Neovim',
'And a bunch more things...'
];
const learning = [
'Astro',
'Figma',
'Smithy',
'Drizzle'
];
const interests = [
'Researching things I need to figure out quickly',
'Ultimate Frisbee',
'Cycling',
'Listening to music (Spotify Wrapped would prove it)',
'Watching movies',
'Slowly getting into reading books',
'Photography',
'Drawing',
'A bit of gaming'
];
---

<BaseLayout pageTitle="Hi!">
  <p>
  I'm Vince, a full-stack developer, full time since 2018, with a university degree in software development and IT engineering. Don't ask why I spent four years in university—the best justification I have is that it gave me more time to tinker before stepping into the industry. Yet, like everyone with a similar experience, I learned more in the first six months of my first job than in those four years of university.
  </p>
  <p>
  I know a bit (enough to do some cool stuff) about:
  </p>
  <ul>
  {skills.map((skill) => (
    <li>{skill}</li>
  ))}
  </ul>
  <p>
  I'm currently learning:
  </p>
  <ul>
  {learning.map((skill) => (
    <li>{skill}</li>
  ))}
  </ul>
  <p>
  I'm a jack of all trades, master of none, but I work with enough different technologies to avoid getting bllsh*tted by magical tech hype while picking up new skills quickly.
  </p>
  <p>
  When I'm not coding. I'm:
  </p>
  <ul>
  {interests.map((skill) => (
    <li>{skill}</li>
  ))}
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
